    "title": "The Artemis II astronauts are flying to the moon — here’s what they’re actually getting paid",
    "text": "NewsNASA's Artemis II moon rocket lifts off from the Kennedy Space Center's Launch Pad39-B Wednesday, April 1, 2026, in Cape Canaveral, Florida.APBy Katherine Rodriguez | NJ.comThe four astronauts on NASA’s Artemis II are currently on their way to the moon after their rocket lifted off from Florida’s Kennedy Space Center Wednesday.But despite the historic and unique nature of their job, they will not be getting any overtime pay.This is because NASA astronauts (and one Canadian Space Agency astronaut) are still government employees, and they make less than you think.Here’s what you need to know about how much these four astronauts make according to NASA and Canadian Space Agency standards.How much do NASA astronauts make per year?According to NASA, U.S. astronauts are paid according to the “general services” pay scale, which is how federal officials determine a government employee’s salary.The agency stated that astronauts should make around $152,258 per year (as of 2024).This figure is adjusted accordingly to reflect increases over the years, and pay is determined by three factors: rigor of the job, tenure and location.While these astronauts may not make much compared to most professions, some are paid more than most government employees, having been classified as GS-13 employees.How much do Canadian Space Agency astronauts make?The Canadian Space Agency has a similar pay structure based on levels for its astronauts: entry level, qualified level and senior level.According to the agency, the salary scale for levels 1-3 ranges from $97,100 to $189,600.Senior level is often reserved for astronauts who have completed a space mission.Despite the pay being relatively low for astronauts in the U.S. and Canada, they are some of the most coveted positions in the world.The last time NASA accepted applications into its applicant pool in 2004, 8,000 people applied for 10 positions, according to NASA’s website.USPS raising prices of stamps by 4 cents, plus 5 other government updates you need to knowArtemis II wake-up music: See the playlist of astronaut wake-up songs on their missionArtemis II astronauts describe seeing dark side of the moon: ‘Something we have never seen before’Katherine Rodriguez is the Trending News Reporter for the Content Innovation Team at NJ.com. For 4+ years now, Katherine has covered trending topics such as travel advisories, TSA-related news, recalls (food,...",
